@page "/HowItWorks/partial-imports"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/partial-imports">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <p>
                The <a href="MainCalculatorPage" class="link-styled">Import data</a> page has a "Load" tick box for each
                type of event: stocks, funds, options, futures, FX, dividends and interest income. Unticking one leaves that
                type out of the files you import. Many events depend on each other, though. Excess reportable income (ERI)
                and equalisation change the cost of a fund holding, equalisation reduces a dividend, and an exercised option
                changes the cost of the shares delivered. If one side is left out, the other side's result is wrong, and
                the app cannot always tell.
            </p>
            <div class="alert alert-info alert-custom" role="note">
                <strong>Recommended: import everything.</strong> Leave every box ticked and import all your statements for
                every year you held the investments, together with your saved session if you have one. The only box you
                may want to untick is "Load FX". Foreign currency trades stand on their own, and whether currency held with
                a broker is chargeable depends on your circumstances (see
                <a href="HowItWorks/foreign-currency" class="link-styled">Foreign currency</a>).
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">What the tick boxes filter</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The tick boxes apply to Interactive Brokers XML files and saved session (JSON) files. FreeTrade,
                    Trading212 and Interactive Investor CSV files always load their trades, dividends and interest,
                    whatever is ticked.
                </li>
                <li>
                    They apply when a file is imported. Unticking a box later does not remove events already imported, and
                    each file you import is added to what is already there.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Corporate actions in a saved session are always loaded. These are stock splits, takeovers, spin-offs,
                    returns of capital, transfers with a spouse or civil partner, ERI and equalisation. From an Interactive
                    Brokers file, stock splits are always loaded and returns of capital only with "Load Dividends".
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">What happens when one side is missing</h2>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-sm">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">You import</th>
                            <th scope="col">But not</th>
                            <th scope="col">What happens</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Fund trades</td>
                            <td>ERI</td>
                            <td>
                                Brokers do not report ERI, so a broker file on its own never has it. The ERI income is missing
                                from your income, and the cost of the fund is not raised, so the gain when you sell is too high
                                by the same amount. The app cannot tell that ERI is missing.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>ERI or equalisation</td>
                            <td>The fund's trades</td>
                            <td>
                                The ERI is still taxed as income, but its cost change goes into an empty Section 104 pool and is
                                picked up by a later purchase. See <a href="HowItWorks/partial-imports#empty-pool"
                                class="link-styled">ERI with no units in the pool</a> below.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>ERI</td>
                            <td>Dividends or interest (box unticked)</td>
                            <td>
                                ERI income is always counted, whatever is ticked, because ERI also changes the fund's cost.
                                Unticking "Load Dividends" or "Load Interest Income" leaves out only broker-reported income.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Equalisation on a dividend or interest payment</td>
                            <td>That dividend or interest</td>
                            <td>
                                An equalisation entry only exists with the income it reduces. If that income is not imported,
                                the entry is left out and a warning lists it. The cost reduction it made to the fund is lost
                                too, so the pool's cost is too high and the gain when you sell too low. Enter it again once the
                                income is there. Equalisation on an ERI entry stays, because ERI is always loaded.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Options</td>
                            <td>Stocks</td>
                            <td>
                                An option that was exercised or assigned needs the share trade that delivered the shares. If it
                                is missing, the import stops with a "No corresponding trade found" error.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Stocks</td>
                            <td>Options</td>
                            <td>
                                Shares bought or sold when an option was exercised are taken at the strike price only. The
                                option premium, which would normally be added to the cost or proceeds, is left out.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Anything</td>
                            <td>FX or futures</td>
                            <td>
                                FX and futures stand on their own. Leaving them out removes their gains and losses and does not
                                change anything else.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example: fund trades without ERI</h2>
                <p>
                    You hold 1,000 units of an Irish ETF costing £10,000. Its reporting period ends on 31 December 2023 with
                    ERI of £0.50 a unit, and on 1 September 2024 you sell all 1,000 units for £12,000.
                </p>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li>
                        <strong>With the ERI entered:</strong> £500 of dividend income in 2024/25, and the pool cost becomes
                        £10,500 on the distribution date, 30 June 2024. The gain on the sale is £12,000 − £10,500 =
                        <strong>£1,500</strong>.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Broker file only:</strong> no ERI income, and a gain of £12,000 − £10,000 =
                        <strong>£2,000</strong>. Your income is £500 too low and your gain £500 too high.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section" id="empty-pool">
            <h2 class="section-title">ERI with no units in the pool</h2>
            <p>
                ERI and equalisation adjust the cost of the units in the Section 104 pool at the end of the reporting
                period. If the imported trades show no units at that date, there is nothing to adjust, but the app still
                changes the pool's cost. The pool then has a cost with no units behind it, and the next purchase of the same
                fund takes that cost on as if it had been paid for those new units.
            </p>
            <p>This happens when:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>"Load Stocks" or "Load Funds" was unticked when you imported the saved session holding the ERI;</li>
                <li>the statements for the years you bought the fund were not imported;</li>
                <li>the ERI was entered for a fund you had already sold before the reporting period end.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The ERI income itself is still taxed, because it was worked out from your holding when you entered it. Only
                the cost is misplaced. Check the <a href="Section104DataPage" class="link-styled">Section 104 pool</a> page:
                a pool showing a cost with 0 units means trades are missing, or the ERI entry should be deleted.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example: ERI without the fund's trades</h2>
                <p>
                    You import the saved session from the example above with "Load Funds" unticked, then import a later
                    statement in which you buy 100 units of the same fund for £1,100 on 1 March 2025 and sell them for £1,300
                    on 1 April 2025.
                </p>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li>The £500 ERI is still taxed as dividend income in 2024/25.</li>
                    <li>On 30 June 2024 the pool holds 0 units with a cost of £500.</li>
                    <li>
                        The March purchase takes the pool to 100 units costing £1,600 instead of £1,100, so the April sale shows
                        a <strong>loss of £300</strong> instead of a <strong>gain of £200</strong>.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Getting a complete result</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    To pick up where you left off, import the saved session with every box ticked. It holds your broker
                    events together with everything entered by hand, so every link between them is restored.
                </li>
                <li>
                    If you start again from broker files, tick every box and import the statements for every year you held
                    the investments. Then enter ERI, equalisation and other corporate actions again. Equalisation is linked
                    to a particular imported income, so it cannot be carried over from a separate session onto freshly
                    imported broker dividends.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Untick "Load FX" if currency gains do not apply to you. Untick other boxes only to leave a type out
                    for good, for example futures you report elsewhere, and only when nothing above depends on it. Do not
                    untick boxes to split an import into parts.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Read the warnings shown after each import. They list any equalisation entries left out.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/reporting-funds" class="link-styled">Excess reportable income and equalisation</a></li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/section-104-pool" class="link-styled">Section 104 pool</a></li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/options" class="link-styled">Options</a></li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/foreign-currency" class="link-styled">Foreign currency</a></li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
